# Frontend Security

The frontend is an untrusted environment.

Never assume data from the browser is trustworthy.

## Secrets

Never expose:

- API secret keys
- database credentials
- Paystack secret keys
- AI provider secret keys
- webhook secrets
- storage secret keys

Only public client-safe configuration may use NEXT_PUBLIC_ variables.

## Authentication

Do not implement authentication security solely through UI hiding.

This:

if (!isAdmin) hide button

is not authorization.

The server must independently authorize every protected operation.

## Authorization

The frontend may hide unavailable actions for UX.

The backend must enforce permissions.

## Financial data

Never trust client-provided:

- price
- subtotal
- discount
- delivery cost
- total
- payment status
- inventory

The server must calculate financial values.

## User-controlled content

Treat all user-generated content as untrusted.

Do not use:

dangerouslySetInnerHTML

unless absolutely necessary and the content has been safely sanitized.

## URLs

Do not construct unsafe redirects from arbitrary user input.

Validate redirect destinations.

## File uploads

Do not trust:

- file extension
- filename
- MIME type supplied by the browser

Server-side validation is required.

## Sensitive data

Do not put sensitive customer data into:

- URLs
- browser logs
- analytics events
- localStorage unnecessarily
- query parameters unnecessarily

## Error messages

Never expose:

- stack traces
- database errors
- internal identifiers unnecessarily
- secret configuration
- internal system details

## AI

AI-generated content is untrusted.

Never render AI output as executable HTML or code.

Never allow AI output to directly control:

- database queries
- shell commands
- payment amounts
- authorization
- inventory
- permissions

AI actions must go through controlled application APIs.